Q.  What is GraphQL? Uses of it.
A.  GraphQL is a query language for your API, and a server-side runtime for executing queries.
    What does “query” mean in GraphQL?
    A query = asking for specific data
    Not just “get data”, but “get exactly this data”
    Example (GraphQL query):
    {
      products {
        title
        price
      }
    }
    👉Meaning: “Give me only title and price of products”

    GraphQL is:
    A query language → you write queries to ask data
    A runtime → server understands & returns exactly that data
    🔹Problem with REST (your example ✔️)
    
    In REST:
    You call /products
    You always get full data (big JSON)
    Even if you need only 2–3 fields

    👉Issues:
    ❌Over-fetching (extra data you don’t need)
    ❌Multiple API calls
    ❌More bandwidth usage
    🔹How GraphQL solves it

    Instead of 3 REST calls, you make custom queries
    1️⃣Home Page
    {
      products {
        title
        description
        price
        rating
        thumbnail
      }
    }
    2️⃣Product Detail Page
    {
      product(id: 1) {
        title
        tags
        images
        discountPercentage
        availabilityStatus
      }
    }
    3️⃣Cart / Order Page
    {
      product(id: 1) {
        title
        tags
        images
        price
        warrantyInformation
        shippingInformation
        availabilityStatus
        brand
      }
    }
    🔹Final Use (in simple words)
    👉GraphQL is used to:
    Fetch only required data
    Reduce API calls
    Improve performance
    Avoid over-fetching & under-fetching.

    🔹One-line difference
    REST → Server decides what you get
    GraphQL → Client decides what it needs
